{
  "policy_id": "ref-17-abolish-ir35-rules",
  "party": "Reform UK",
  "policy_title": "Abolish IR35 rules",
  "outcome": "O4",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O4",
    "direction": "mixed",
    "magnitude": "moderate",
    "time_horizon": "this-parliament",
    "confidence": "moderate",
    "plain": "Abolishing IR35 would boost take-home pay and opportunities for genuine freelancers and contractors, but would also remove the main rule stopping employers from reclassifying workers as self-employed to avoid giving them sick pay, holiday pay, and pensions. The net effect depends on how many workers are genuinely self-employed versus how many would lose rights.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether abolition would primarily liberate genuine contractors or accelerate 'bogus self-employment', stripping employment rights from workers who currently have them.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy aims to abolish IR35 rules to support sole traders, recognising they work longer hours and take more risks without pension or sick pay.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "Reform UK will abolish IR35 rules to support sole traders, acknowledging that self-employed individuals often work longer hours and take more risks without pension or sick pay benefits."
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "IR35 rules exist to ensure contractors pay broadly the same tax as employees, countering tax avoidance.",
        "evidence_id": "E1",
        "quote": "The legislation aims to ensure these workers pay broadly the same Income Tax and National Insurance Contributions (NICs) as employees, thereby countering tax avoidance"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The 2017 and 2021 reforms raised around \u00a34.2 billion in additional tax and NICs in three years.",
        "evidence_id": "E8",
        "quote": "HMRC estimated that the 2017 and 2021 reforms raised around \u00a34.2 billion in additional tax and NICs in the three years to March 2023"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Contractors deemed inside IR35 can see their take-home pay cut by up to 30%, and some businesses have imposed blanket bans on contractors.",
        "evidence_id": "E14",
        "quote": "the current rules have led to \"blanket bans\" on contractors by some businesses or an increase in \"inside IR35\" determinations, limiting opportunities and reducing take-home pay by up to 30% for those deemed inside IR35"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "A typical self-employed worker pays approximately 55% less in total labour tax than an employee on the same gross pay, representing an 11-percentage-point tax gap.",
        "evidence_id": "E7",
        "quote": "a typical self-employed worker pays approximately 55% less in total labour tax than an employee earning the same gross pay (2025-26 estimate), representing an 11-percentage-point tax gap in gross income"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Abolition could attract skilled contractors back to UK contracting and increase business willingness to engage contractors.",
        "evidence_id": "E13",
        "quote": "It could attract skilled professionals back to contracting in the UK who may have moved overseas or left the profession due to the current rules"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Abolition could distort the labour market by making contractor engagement significantly cheaper than employment.",
        "evidence_id": "E16",
        "quote": "This could distort the labour market by making contractor engagement significantly cheaper for businesses than employment"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Abolishing IR35 would mean more workers treated as self-employed without employment rights such as sick pay, holiday pay, and pension contributions.",
        "evidence_id": "E17",
        "quote": "abolishing IR35 would mean more workers are treated as self-employed for tax purposes, without granting them the employment rights (e.g., sick pay, holiday pay, pension contributions) that employees receive"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "The CIOT has highlighted the problem of 'zero-rights employees' \u2014 a class of workers with employment-like relationships but no employment-law protections.",
        "evidence_id": "E18",
        "quote": "The Chartered Institute of Taxation (CIOT) has previously highlighted the \"unacceptable\" distinction between employment status for tax and employment law, which has created a class of \"zero-rights employees\""
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "The IFS and OTS argue the core issue is differential tax treatment and a comprehensive reform is needed rather than simply removing IR35.",
        "evidence_id": "E27",
        "quote": "the IFS and OTS, suggest that the core issue lies in the differential tax treatment of employment and self-employment, and a more comprehensive reform of the tax system is needed rather than simply removing IR35 without an alternative"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "The estimated cost of the short-lived 2022 repeal was \u00a36.19 billion over five years, and the Resolution Foundation estimated around \u00a32 billion a year.",
        "evidence_id": "E6",
        "quote": "The estimated cost of that repeal was \u00a36.19 billion over five years"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "Abolishing IR35 has clear, evidence-supported upsides for genuine sole traders and contractors: up to 30% higher take-home pay for those currently caught inside IR35, fewer blanket bans by hirers, and a more accessible market for skilled freelancers. These are real improvements to pay levels and job quality for that group. However, the same abolition removes the main legislative barrier to employers reclassifying workers as self-employed \u2014 stripping them of sick pay, holiday pay, and pension contributions without granting corresponding rights. The CIOT's warning about 'zero-rights employees' and the Resolution Foundation's evidence on the large tax gap between employment and self-employment both point to a serious risk: in the absence of IR35, the labour market incentive for businesses to push workers into self-employed arrangements (saving ~11 percentage points of labour tax) grows substantially. For workers in weaker bargaining positions, this could mean worse conditions and lower effective security even if nominal pay is maintained. The verdict is therefore genuinely mixed. Genuine contractors benefit materially; workers on the margin of employment status risk losing rights. The magnitude depends on which group is larger and how aggressively firms exploit the new incentive. The IFS and OTS suggest abolition without broader tax system reform leaves the structural problem unresolved. There is also a fiscal cost of roughly \u00a32 billion per year (projected), which constrains the government's ability to fund other worker-support measures. On balance, the policy improves conditions for clearly self-employed people but worsens protections for those who could be reclassified \u2014 a genuine mixed outcome at moderate magnitude.",
