      "notes": "The verdict is well-structured and evidence-grounded. The mixed direction is genuinely supported by claims on both sides: concrete benefits to genuine contractors (pay restoration, reduced blanket bans) and concrete risks to workers vulnerable to reclassification (loss of employment rights). Tier assignments are appropriate \u2014 the 30% pay cut and tax gap figures are cited from sources that present them as observed/estimated facts, which is defensible as measurable. E14's source (rootsinsurance.co.uk/merje.com) is advocacy-adjacent but the claim is hedged ('up to 30%') and corroborated by multiple sources. E18's CIOT claim is labelled projected, which is slightly generous \u2014 it's more of an analytical/expert observation \u2014 but not a blocking issue. The fiscal cost claim (E6) correctly cites only the \u00a36.19bn figure from E6 and acknowledges E9 separately; no overstatement. No party identity leakage detected. No lever scored for its own sake \u2014 all claims are routed through employment rights, pay security, and job quality. Magnitude 'moderate' and time horizon 'this-parliament' are plausible given the scale of the reform and near-term labour market effects. No blocking issues found."
    },
